WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated performer

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:05.104
and best-selling author
you know from her shows

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:08.140
like "Strangers with Candy"
and "At Home with Amy Sedaris."

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.177
You can see her in the second
season of "Girls5eva,"

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:12.612
which is streaming now
on Peacock.

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:13.846
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:15.881
-Your father and I
walked the devil's path,

00:00:15.881 --> 00:00:17.483
and it was a dark one.

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:20.286
-We had no moral compass,
no regard for tomorrow.

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:21.921
Just dopamine, thrills!

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:24.957
-Waking up in a truck bed
beside a civic center,

00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:28.862
kind of all wet with, like,
coins all stuck to me.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:31.097
-[ Laughter ]
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.433
our dear friend Amy Sedaris!

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:35.101
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:41.507 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:47.914 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:49.082
Hi! How are you?

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.551
-I'm good. Thank you.
Thanks for having me on.

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:54.921
-It's so great to see you again.
-It's really good to see you.

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:56.456
And no jacket. I like this.
-I know!

00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.257
I got a little more casual
over a pandemic.

00:00:58.257 --> 00:01:01.394
Yeah. Yeah, I was like,
"Nothing matters." Yeah.

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:02.929
It's really nice
to have you back.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:04.530
-Thank you.
-One of the things we --

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.265
Why did you bring out a mug,
by the way?

00:01:06.265 --> 00:01:07.600
-I'm gonna get you
to sign it later

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.135
for an auction I'm doing,
which I'll get to.

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:10.837
-Okay, great. You can feel free
to set it down.

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:11.905
-Okay, okay.
-Okay, great.

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:13.306
-Oh, yeah,
you don't give water here.

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:14.974
Do you have water?
-Yeah, we have water here.

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:16.242
-Oh, look at that jug.
Oh, I see.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:18.111
Well, you don't want
to fill it up too much,

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:19.679
'cause you're gonna
have to sign it.

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.181
-Okay, yeah.
That's a good point.

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:22.915
But, you know,
we've always talked about

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:26.052
how your home is full
of wonderful objects.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:27.586
-Oh, have we talked about that?
-We have.

00:01:27.586 --> 00:01:29.689
-I have been decluttering
like crazy.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.624
-Okay.
-It's been very stressful.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.827
I don't know. Having to say
goodbye to things.

00:01:33.827 --> 00:01:36.129
Things I never thought I'd get
rid of, I'm getting rid of.

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:37.963
-Okay.
-Yeah, a lot of anxiety.

00:01:37.963 --> 00:01:40.099
I lost 6 pounds doing it.

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:42.368
You know, and I had some friends
helping me, and they were like,

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:43.869
"You know, you have to
make a quick decision.

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:46.072
Are you gonna keep it,
are you gonna get rid of it?"

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:47.540
It's hard.
-And you have some photos

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.842
of some of the things. Now, I
think after you gave that setup,

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:51.677
people might be surprised
by some of these items,

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:53.713
because I think no one here
would keep this.

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:54.947
-Really?! What?!

00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:56.416
-Well, let's start
with some of your books.

00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:59.651
This is a book called
"Asthma and Eczema."

00:01:59.651 --> 00:02:01.121
-Yeah, I don't have --

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:03.322
That's a page-turner.
It's really good.

00:02:03.322 --> 00:02:06.024
It's a little dry.

00:02:06.024 --> 00:02:08.628
Yeah, I'm giving it away.

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.062
-You gave it away?
-I'm gonna give it away.

00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:11.497
-You're going to give it away.

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:13.366
-The auction is gonna be --
You know Cure Thrift Store?

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:15.001
It's kind of in our
neighborhood, 3rd and 13th.

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:17.036
And we're gonna do
an online auction.

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:18.271
-Oh, great.

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:20.506
-And all the money
goes to diabetes research.

00:02:20.506 --> 00:02:22.474
Plus, I'll probably
throw in some rabbit,

00:02:22.474 --> 00:02:24.443
you know, places in there.
-Gotcha.

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:26.445
-And then my brother
is doing a --

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:28.014
He has a new book coming out,

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:30.082
and June 2nd, he's doing
an event in Brooklyn.

00:02:30.082 --> 00:02:31.483
And I can give away --

00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:33.886
I can just give away stuff
on a display table.

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:35.955
So I have three bins of,
like, palm stuff.

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.391
Like, a wooden mushroom.
You know, things like that.

00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:40.159
-This is another book
that I can imagine.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:44.597
This one's harder to part with,
because this seems so useful.

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:46.933
-Oh, yeah.
-"Microwave for One."

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:49.135
-Well, I don't have a microwave.
-Well, there you go.

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:50.703
-So that's why
I'm giving that away.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.305
-So it seems
like of all your books,

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:53.705
you got rid of the ones

00:02:53.705 --> 00:02:57.009
where you don't have
a microwave, asthma, or eczema.

00:02:57.009 --> 00:02:59.379
-You never know,
when guests come over.

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:02.181
-What is this?
Are you getting rid of this?

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:04.583
-Okay, that is
a rusty-nail wind chime

00:03:04.583 --> 00:03:07.120
that Todd Ola made for me.
[ LIGHT LAUGHTER ]

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.122
And it was for people
with bipolar.

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:11.657
You know, that way, when you're
in the upswing, downswing.

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:14.160
It's about crafts for people
with that disability.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.662
So maybe when you're depressed,
you take a handful of nails

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:18.264
and you throw them
in your backyard.

00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:20.032
-Okay.
-And then, when spring comes,

00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:22.302
you go get those nails,
and they'll have rust on them.

00:03:22.302 --> 00:03:23.702
-Okay.
-Which is pretty.

00:03:23.702 --> 00:03:25.171
And then you hang them up.

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:27.373
But the problem is now,
with nails, is it galvanized?

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.208
Is that how you say it?
They don't rust.

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:32.578
So I had to go on eBay
and get these rusty nails.

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:33.880
-Sorry.
So you went on eBay

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.115
and you got pre-rusted nails?
-Yes.

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:39.385
There's a lot of guys
selling rusty nails on eBay.

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:41.887
And I paid $7,
and he sent me a lot.

00:03:41.887 --> 00:03:43.422
-Okay, what kind of guy --

00:03:43.422 --> 00:03:46.092
When you picture a guy selling
rusty nails, what do you think?

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:49.294
-Ooh. [ Laughs ]

00:03:49.294 --> 00:03:50.663
Yeah. You know.

00:03:50.663 --> 00:03:52.932
-This is a --
Is this a clipboard?

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:54.400
-Yes!
-Okay.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.603
-The problem with that clipboard
is that it's too long.

00:03:57.603 --> 00:04:00.606
You know what I mean?
It's too long for my body.

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:02.742
You know, hold it up,
and it just comes -- You know.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:06.179
-So the problem is not that it
has a human intestine drawing?

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:07.347
-No.
-Okay.

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:10.182
-I'm gonna get
big bucks for that. Yeah.

00:04:10.182 --> 00:04:11.784
-What are these?
-Children's crutches!

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.319
Because I have them because --

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.187
When people come to my house
and they have kids,

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:17.122
I'm like, "Do you want to play
with the crutches?"

00:04:17.122 --> 00:04:18.590
And let me tell you --

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:21.393
Nobody wants to see their kids
playing with crutches.

00:04:21.393 --> 00:04:22.862
-Uh-huh.

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.797
So, you have these just for --
not for kids who are injured.

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:26.666
You have these for healthy kids.
-To play in.

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:28.233
Yeah, they're playing
with crutches.

00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:31.570
-They look like they're
from a bygone era, as well.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:32.772
-Yeah, they're pretty bad.

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:34.473
-Those are like
"Little Women" crutches.

00:04:34.473 --> 00:04:36.909
-Yeah. [ Laughs ] It's so true.
-And now, this is interesting.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:38.210
Do you think this will move?

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:40.780
-Oh, it says "JEW."
It's the initials of somebody.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.982
Julie Ellen Worden.
-Okay.

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:45.952
-But it says "JEW," so,
hopefully a Jewish person.

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:49.789
-Yeah. Either someone with
those initials or, more likely,

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:51.757
a Jewish person
would be drawn to it.

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:56.229
I also want to ask
about Tina real quick.

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:57.696
-Oh, yes.

00:04:57.696 --> 00:04:59.599
-We've talked about your
rabbit, Tina, over the years.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:01.501
It should be noted, one of
the first times you were on,

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:04.069
you took Tina to the vet,
and Tina was a boy.

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:07.006
-Yes, after four years.
-Okay, so, there's Tina.

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:09.041 line:0%
-Look at Tina the other day.
Look at that leg.

00:05:09.041 --> 00:05:12.177 line:0%
Yeah. No, Tina, you know,
when I got her, the first --

00:05:12.177 --> 00:05:13.846
I did your show,
and I had just gotten Tina.

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:16.048
What is it, 9 years ago
or 10 years ago?

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:18.617
My problem is that people
that watch Tina for me

00:05:18.617 --> 00:05:22.321
when I go away on big jobs,
they're moving.

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:23.623
-Oh, no!
-So now I'm really --

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:25.291
I go to bed like this
because I'm worried

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:27.326
I have to find somebody
who can watch Tina for me.

00:05:27.326 --> 00:05:30.329
-Is it a lot of work
to rabbit-sit Tina?

00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:31.897
-Well, no, most rabbits --

00:05:31.897 --> 00:05:34.567
I mean, just in case, you know,
rabbits can get sick like that

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.735
because they don't speak
like most animals.

00:05:36.735 --> 00:05:39.271
They try to communicate
through their actions.

00:05:39.271 --> 00:05:41.173
So I really want someone
who knows about rabbits.

00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:43.142
-'Cause your current
babysitter's like, "No."

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:45.177
-Yeah, I met them
at a rabbit conference. Yeah.

00:05:45.177 --> 00:05:46.612
-Okay, that's great.

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:48.781
-Which they don't do anymore
in New Rochelle.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:50.416
-They canceled the --
-Yes!

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:52.084
-You met them at a real
rabbit conference?

00:05:52.084 --> 00:05:53.218
-Yes.
-Oh, my God.

00:05:53.218 --> 00:05:55.220
-But now I speak to them
on "Thump" magazine,

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:58.057
which is another -- Yes.

00:05:58.057 --> 00:05:59.959
-You subscribe to "Thump"?
-Yes.

00:05:59.959 --> 00:06:01.360
-And people do
look like their --

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:02.995
At rabbit conferences,
when people say,

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:04.530
"Oh, people look
like their animals,"

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.733
it's, like,
everybody's got like...

00:06:07.733 --> 00:06:09.501
buck teeth.

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:11.103
-There's a carrot bar?

00:06:11.103 --> 00:06:13.071
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.071 --> 00:06:14.907
-Oh, that's fun, yeah.

00:06:14.907 --> 00:06:17.042
Well, I used to sell stuff,
but they don't buy things.

00:06:17.042 --> 00:06:18.410
Rabbit owners don't.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:19.845
-They don't buy things?
-They're pretty cheap.

00:06:19.845 --> 00:06:21.380
-Did you find anything
while you were decluttering

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:23.349
that was too dear to get rid of?

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:25.517 line:0%
-Lots of stuff, which --
I brought this mug out

00:06:25.517 --> 00:06:27.687 line:0%
'cause you gotta sign this
'cause I can put this in a --

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:29.388 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:31.724
See, I told you!

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.259
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:35.461
I'm sorry. Oops!

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:37.129
-Are you sor--
How can you be sorry?

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:39.364
That was the greatest payoff.

00:06:39.364 --> 00:06:42.268
I feel like I was
just in a Hitchcock movie.

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:43.803
[ Laughter ]

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:46.172
-Because I have Letterman's --
I complimented Letterman once.

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:47.807
He had tassels on his loafers.

00:06:47.807 --> 00:06:49.575
He ripped them off
and he gave them to me.

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:51.110
-Really?!
-But I'm gonna keep those.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:52.812
-Oh, you're gonna keep
the tassels, but --

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:54.180
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:57.215
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.215 --> 00:06:59.785
And Martha Stewart
is having a tag sale, also.

00:06:59.785 --> 00:07:01.353
-Really?
-Same time I am.

00:07:01.353 --> 00:07:02.688
Yeah, competition's on.

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:04.790
-Hold on.
I'm gonna sign that for you.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:07.125
-And when I did
"Conan O'Brien" once, I had,

00:07:07.125 --> 00:07:10.696
like, a wood carved squirrel,
and I turned it into, you know,

00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:12.965
to smoke pot out of,
like a bong.

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:15.635
And I brought it on "Conan,"
and I handed it to Martha,

00:07:15.635 --> 00:07:17.336
and it still had pot
in the chamber.

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:19.639
I'm auctioning that off.
-Really?

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:21.740
-Yeah.
-But she didn't ever smoke it?

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.676
You just gave it to her.
-I don't know.

00:07:23.676 --> 00:07:25.945
[ Laughter ]

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.546
Yeah.

00:07:27.546 --> 00:07:29.348
-You -- We were talking
about rabbit-sitting,

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:30.816
and I do want to ask.
-Okay.

00:07:30.816 --> 00:07:34.253
-Were you -- As a younger
person, did you ever babysit?

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:36.055
-I was a horrible babysitter.
-Why were you horrible?

00:07:36.055 --> 00:07:37.523
I'm surprised.
-I was real --

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:40.225
A minute the parents left,
I was like, "You go to bed now.

00:07:40.225 --> 00:07:42.828
I don't care what --
You go now, right now."

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:44.263
And then I'd make them
go to bed.

00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:46.765
Of course, I'd eat everything,
ransack the place.

00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:48.601
And then I was addicted
to Barbra Streisand,

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.035
and that's back in Rona Barrett.

00:07:50.035 --> 00:07:51.537
You know,
they had those magazines.

00:07:51.537 --> 00:07:54.473
I would, you know, cut up
pictures of Barbra Streisand

00:07:54.473 --> 00:07:55.974
that were in
the gossip magazines.

00:07:55.974 --> 00:07:57.976
-So you would go
through their home --

00:07:57.976 --> 00:08:00.513
-Ransack it.
-And you would cut out --

00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:02.415
I mean, I would say,
there might be nothing more

00:08:02.415 --> 00:08:04.550
haunting than dropping off
the babysitter,

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.885
coming back home, flipping
through your magazines,

00:08:06.885 --> 00:08:10.389
and just, like, all the
Barbra Streisands are gone.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:13.358
-I know. It's terrible.
-Yeah, well --

00:08:13.358 --> 00:08:15.695
-Or I'd double-book.
I'd say yes to a lot of people

00:08:15.695 --> 00:08:18.130
because I didn't want
to turn people down.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.799
-That's good.
People do like to find out

00:08:19.799 --> 00:08:21.734
at the last minute
the babysitter is not coming.

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.002
-"Yes. Sorry.

00:08:23.002 --> 00:08:25.037
But I'm sending someone
named Rikki over."

00:08:25.037 --> 00:08:26.305
And that never worked out.

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:27.773
But I wanted to talk
about this dress.

00:08:27.773 --> 00:08:31.243
When I go shopping with David --
-Yes, that's your brother.

00:08:31.243 --> 00:08:33.045
-My brother David.

00:08:33.045 --> 00:08:35.114
He talked me into getting this
dress because it's unflattering.

00:08:35.114 --> 00:08:38.351
-Okay.
-And the tulle is up here.

00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:40.686
And one time,
I talked Dave into buying a pair

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:45.391
of women's slacks at Agnes B,
where it zipped up the back.

00:08:45.391 --> 00:08:47.326
And he was at a urinal.

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:49.161
[ Laughter ]

00:08:49.161 --> 00:08:51.430
I didn't want to -- So I might
auction this dress off.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.065
-It's beautiful.
I like that dress.

00:08:53.065 --> 00:08:55.334
I don't think it's unflattering.
-I feel like I'm pregnant.

00:08:55.334 --> 00:08:58.103
That's what it feels like.
-I guess it doesn't look --

00:08:58.103 --> 00:09:00.105
I see that there's
some flaws, yeah.

00:09:00.105 --> 00:09:01.873
-Yeah, he goes, "Oh..."
-But when you walked out,

00:09:01.873 --> 00:09:03.208
oh, my goodness. Yeah.

00:09:03.208 --> 00:09:06.345
It was, like, up here,
and as you sat, it was like...

00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:09.281
-I'm gonna auction it off.
Byoo-oop! Yeah. Whatever.

00:09:09.281 --> 00:09:10.950
